## Supporting Information Placeholder
Ovarian cancer has the lowest survival rate of all gynecologic malignancies, largely because late diagnosis mitigates against effective therapeutics.1 Lysophosphatidic acid (LPA, Scheme 1), a bioactive phospholipid, exhibits pleiomorphic functions in multiple cell lineages and stimulates the proliferation of cancer cells. Accompanied by a complicated cascade of genetic, molecular, and biochemical events, LPA levels are consistently elevated in plasma suggesting that LPA plays a role in the pathophysiology of ovarian cancer.2 Moreover, increased levels of LPA, altered receptor expression in ovarian carcinogenesis and metastasis demonstrate markedly different responses to LPA than most other epithelial tumors. Therefore, the rapid detection of plasma LPA levels could represent a promising approach to diagnose this devastating disease during its early stages. However, the selective and sensitive detection of LPA is currently limited due to expensive, sophisticated devices and complicated procedures, including tandem mass spectroscopy, capillary electrophoresis and radio-enzymatic assays.3 Fluorometric techniques could offer facile, sensitive and fast assays and would likely be preferred if available. We address this matter herein through the
study of a family of isostructural lanthanide metal-organic frameworks (MOFs).
MOFs or porous coordination polymers (PCPs)4 have attracted great attention for their high permanent porosity coupled with structural tunability, and hence show potential for utility in hydrogen storage,5 gas separation,6 enhanced catalysis,7 smart sensors,8 and drug delivery9. Lanthanide MOFs (Ln-MOFs) offer the possibility of exploiting the luminescent properties of lanthanide cations such as long emission lifetimes with sharp and characteristic line emissions.10 In addition, the luminescent intensity of Ln-MOFs can be tuned by the host-guest chemistry, therefore providing an opportunity for chemical sensing of molecules such as LPA.
Ln-MOFs with zeolite-like topologies can exhibit high porosity, stability and tunable composition. Large pore diameters and high specific pore volume enable guest molecules, even relatively large molecules like LPA, to interact with the host framework. Although zeolite-like MOFs (ZMOFs) are already known for transition metals,11 lanthanide ZMOFs (Ln-ZMOFs) are understudied, presumably because their high coordination numbers and diverse coordination environments of makes it difficult to design suitable 4-connected nodes. We herein demonstrate that judicious selection of luminescent \(\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}\) centers and ligands to form secondary building units (SBUs)12 enables construction of a family of Ln-ZMOFs with zeolitic topology. Specifically, tetrahedral
building units (TBUs) made by reacting \(2,2^{\prime}\)-bipyridine-6,6'-dicarboxylic \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpcd}\right)\) with \(\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) in methanol/chloroform at \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) afford colorless polyhedral crystals of \(\left[\mathrm{Tb}_{48}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{48}(\mathrm{bpcd})_{48}\right]\cdot\mathrm{G}_{\mathrm{x}}\) (Tb-ZMOF; \(\mathrm{G}=\) guest) with rho topology. Eu-ZMOF was obtained using the same procedure using by \(\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\). Mixed crystal variants \(\mathrm{Eu}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Tb}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\)-ZMOF were synthesized by varying \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) stoichiometry.
Single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis reveals that Tb-ZMOF crystallizes in the cubic space group \(P n{-}3n\). There is one crystallographically independent \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ion, which is coordinated by two nitrogen atoms from one \(\mathrm{bpdc}^{2}\), four oxygen atoms from three other \(\mathrm{bpdc}^{2-}\) ligands and two oxygen atoms from a nitrate ion. Each \(\mathrm{bpdc}^{2-}\) anion acts as both a linker and a chelator and coordinates to three \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ions. The eight-coordinate \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) complex thereby generated serves as a TBU as shown in Figure 1(a). Self-assembly of these TBUs affords truncated cuboctahedral cages (\(\alpha\)-cages), which when further linked through double-eight-membered rings generate \(\beta\)-cages and rho topology MOF (Figure 1(b)). Unlike previous rho-ZMOFs, carboxylate groups instead of nitrogen donors direct the framework topology. Tb-N bonds serve an ancillary role by completing the 8-coordinate geometry. ZMOFs that exhibit rho topology have been observed for \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{nd}\) and \(\mathrm{In}^{3+}\). To our knowledge, Tb-ZMOF represents the first example of a zeolite-like lanthanide MOF with rho-topology. Given that TBU of Tb-ZMOF is eight times larger than that of zeolite RHO, the \(\alpha\)-cage is \(17.6\,\mathrm{\AA}\) in diameter. The packing of the \(\alpha\)- and \(\beta\)-cages is evident from a tiling illustration (Figure 1(c)). The solvent-accessible volume of 1 is \(37.5\%\) when calculated using PLATON.
Varying the molar ratio of \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) resulted in mixed crystal variants \(\mathrm{Eu}_{\mathrm{x}}\mathrm{Tb}_{1-\mathrm{x}}\)-ZMOF: \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.2206}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.7794}\)-ZMOF (MZMOF-1), \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.3525}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.6415}\)-ZMOF (MZMOF-2) and \(\mathrm{Eu}_{0.6059}\mathrm{Tb}_{0.3941}\)-ZMOF (MZMOF-3) as determined by inductively coupled plasma atom emission spectroscopy (ICPAES). As expected, each framework is isostructural with Tb-ZMOF according to powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) diffra
tograms (Figure S1). The void spaces of Tb-ZMOF, Eu-ZMOF and the MZMOFs are occupied by guest molecules which can be removed by heating (Figure S2). TG curves showed an initial weight loss from 25 to 170 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), corresponding to the release of guest molecules. No clear weight loss was observed between 170 and 410 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), indicating high degree of thermal stability.
The photoluminescence (PL) spectra of Tb-ZMOF, Eu-ZMOF and MZMOFs suspended in methanol were collected in the 400–700 nm range when excited at 300 nm (Figure 2). The characteristic lanthanide luminescence was observed with sharp and well-resolved emission peaks. Tb-ZMOF displays emission bands at 488, 546, 583, and 621 nm in the visible region coming from the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}\) \((J=6, 5, 4\), and 3) transitions of \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) ions. Characteristic transitions of the \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions ascribed to \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{J}\) \((J=1, 2\), and 3) transitions are evident for Eu-ZMOF at 589, 616, and 653 nm. As expected, MZMOFs simultaneously produce the characteristic emission bands of both \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions and their respective emission intensities decrease and increase as composition varies. Therefore, we have modulated fluorescent performance of the individual lanthanide ions by controlling the ratio of the two metals during synthesis.
In order to establish the performance of Tb-ZMOF, Eu-ZMOF and the three MZMOFs towards LPA, the fluorescent emission responses were collected using an LPA solution in MeOH. Specifically, the fluorescence spectrum of each material suspended in MeOH (2 mL) was recorded, to this was added a 0.05 mM solution of LPA (0.5 and 1 mL) and the resulting emissions were monitored. The intensities of the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\rightarrow{}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{5}\) \((I_{\mathrm{Tb}}=546\ \mathrm{nm})\) and \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{0}{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}\) \((I_{\mathrm{Eu}}=613\ \mathrm{nm})\) transitions were used for the calculation of intensity change by \((I-I_{\mathrm{o}})/I_{\mathrm{o}}\), where \(I_{\mathrm{o}}\) is the initial fluorescence intensity before the addition of analyte and \(I\) is the fluorescence intensity in the presence of analyte. As shown in Figures 3 and S3-7, the emission intensities of Tb-ZMOF suspensions decrease gradually upon incremental addition of LPA (0.05 mM). In contrast, fluorescence enhancement of Eu-ZMOF suspensions was observed, an 8% increase vs. the initial fluorescence intensity. Further addition of LPA led to a lower enhancement of fluorescence intensity, which was most pronounced in dilute Eu-ZMOF
suspensions. Mixed crystal MZMOFs exhibited similar behavior to that of Tb-ZMOF and Eu-ZMOF. The intensity ratio of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) (integrated intensity, \(I=I_{\mathrm{Eu}}/I_{\mathrm{Tb}}\)) correlated well with the concentration of LPA. It was found that the best sensitivity towards LPA was exhibited by MZMOF-3, \(82\%\) enhancement of integrated intensity.
Compounds present in blood plasma might interfere with detection of LPA. In order to mimic blood plasma, water, glucose \((4.5\mathrm{~-~}5.5\mathrm{~mM})\), \(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\) \((136\mathrm{~-~}146\mathrm{~mM})\), Cl\(^{-}\) \((96\mathrm{~-~}106\;\mathrm{mM})\), L-proline \((3\mathrm{~-~}5\;\mathrm{mM})\), and urea \((3.5\mathrm{~-~}7.0\;\mathrm{mM})\) were prepared as MeOH solutions. The luminescent intensities of Tb-ZMOF, Eu-ZMOF and MZMOF suspensions in the presence of upper limit concentrations of these major plasma components were determined (Figures 3 and S3-7). For Tb-ZMOF and Eu-ZMOF, these additives were found to exhibit different behaviors but in general they exhibit fluorescent emission signals comparable to that of LPA. However, MZMOFs were found to exhibit different selectivity for LPA over the other analytes. There were negligible effects on fluorescence intensity with MZMOF-1 and MZMOF-2 suspensions. Slightly increased relative luminescent intensities were observed in MZMOF-3 when compared to MZMOF-1 and MZMOF-2 but with 10 - 80 times lower response than that of LPA. Fluorescent titration of different concentrations of LPA in the presence of a mixture of L-proline \((5\;\mathrm{mM})\), urea \((7\;\mathrm{mM})\), NaCl \((4.6\;\mathrm{mM})\) and glucose \((5.5\;\mathrm{mM})\) was also studied (Figures S14-S16). Lower intensities (ca. \(30\%\)) than pure LPA were observed but, nevertheless, a good linear relationship was retained (Figure S17).
Physiological concentrations of LPA in plasma typically vary from \(0.1-6.3\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\) whereas danger levels for ovarian cancer are indicated by concentrations on the order of \(43.1\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\). In order to investigate the performance of MZMOF-3 for detecting LPA within that range, fluorescence intensity was monitored upon incremental addition \((0.1\;\mathrm{mL})\) of \(0.05\;\mathrm{mM}\) LPA solution (Figure S9). The fluorescence titration results are visualized by plotting the integrated fluorescence intensity versus real-time concentration of LPA in MZMOF-3 sus
pension (Figure S12b). The plot revealed that there is a linear relationship with a slope of \(0.05000\;\pm\;0.00179\), indicating that MZMOF-3 is a self-referencing fluorescence sensor. Fluorescence titrations were also performed with 0.03, 0.1 and \(0.13\;\mathrm{mM}\) LPA, respectively (Figure S8, S10 and S11). The integrated intensity was observed to linearly correlate to the concentration of LPA (Figure S12). Indeed, the slopes obtained from each curve are indistinguishable. Furthermore, four individual measurements were integrated into one complete plot with a slope of \(0.04852\;\pm\;0.00051\), as shown in Figure 4 and S12e. Accordingly, fluorescence intensity from LPA in MZMOF-3 suspension is quantitative in the concentration range from 1.4 to \(43.3\;\upmu\mathrm{M}\), which reflects typical physiological levels. Further, the data indicate that intensity is independent of the amount of MZMOF-3, the only correlation being to LPA concentration. MZMOF-3 is therefore a self-calibrating fluorescent indicator and does not require additional calibration of luminescence intensity.
Figure 4. Integrated luminescent intensity changes of MZMOF-3 suspended in MeOH towards o.o3, o.1 and o.13 mM LPA.
The fluorescence response of MZMOF-3 in water (o.3 \(\mathrm{mg/mL}\) water suspension) upon addition of LPA (o.10 mM aqueous solution) was also studied. Negligible effect on integrated fluorescent intensity was observed (Figure S13). This could be the result of competition with water for binding sites within MZMOF-3 or aggregation of LPA to form micelles. However, lyophilized plasma reconstituted by MeOH would represent a viable approach to LPA detection.
The fluorescent emission properties of MZMOF-3 with respect to LPA suggest an energy transfer mechanism, i.e. the increased intensity of \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) transitions and the decreased intensity of \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) transitions induced by energy transfer from \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions were significantly enhanced in the presence of LPA. To address this matter, the lifetime of MZMOF-3 towards LPA was monitored at 546 and \(613~\mathrm{nm}\), respectively (Figure S18). The fluorescent lifetime of \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{4}\) \((\tau_{\mathrm{Tb}})\) is reduced by the addition of LPA (o.5 to \(1~\mathrm{mL}\)), whereas the \({}^{5}\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{o}}{\rightarrow}^{7}\mathrm{F}_{2}\) \((\tau_{\mathrm{Eu}})\) lifetime increased (Table S2). This is consistent with an energy transfer mechanism from \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions in the presence of LPA. On the contrary, the lifetimes of both cations in MZMOF-1 and MZMOF-2 increase as LPA are added. The effect of LPA is consistent with intermolecular interactions between LPA and MZMOF-3. Notably, LPA and GPA (glycerophosphoric acid) have the same head phosphate and 2-sn-OH functional groups but very different end groups (Scheme 1) yet they exhibit different fluorescent behavior towards MZMOF-3 (Figure 3). We presume that the oleoyl acid chain in combination with the phosphate of LPA generates different hydrogen bonding and/or hydrophobic interactions, which might account for the magnitude of its luminescent intensity and its affinity towards MZMOF-3.
In conclusion, we report single step synthesis of lanthanide zeolite-like metal-organic frameworks, Tb-ZMOF and EuZMOF, which are based upon four-connected single-lanthanide-ion-based TBUs. Isostructural mixed crystal MZMOFs prepared by varying the molar ratios of \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) to \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) exhibit the characteristic emission bands of both \(\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}\) and \(\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}\) ions. MZMOF-3 was found to exhibit selective detection of LPA, which is a biomarker of ovarian cancer, even in the presence of major components of blood plasma. To our knowledge, this is the first example of a MOF that senses trace amounts of LPA at biologically relevant concentrations. MZMOF-3 not only exhibits high sensitivity and selectivity to LPA, but it is self-referencing and self-calibrating. These results suggest a new general route to create luminescent mixed crystal Ln-MOFs that might enable early detection of disease or be suitable for other biochemical sensing applications.
